**Accepted API-only boundary.** The external project package surface is
implemented. It cannot become an application entry through generic directives.

A project can expose a public package API to other projects through an optional
`+*.moth` root at the selected project directory.

### What the package surface does

A `+*.moth` root at the project root is the project package's support surface.
It exports declarations that external consumers can bind. Without it, the
project has no externally consumable package surface. When an `@*.moth` normal
root is also present, both roots share helpers but keep isolated authoring
scopes and interfaces.

The support surface rejects top-level runtime work and page fragments. It
cannot declare root-only `$page` metadata, has no implicit entry and emits no
route.

### How it reaches descendant modules

The support surface follows the ordinary support visibility rule. It does not
receive a privileged namespace over descendant modules, and internal project
modules cannot import it as a private back door.

### Privacy and inputs

It cannot depend on `@project` or expose declarations that depend on
project-private context. Configuration input contracts stay scoped to one
project or package boundary; a consumer doesn't satisfy a dependency's
contracts implicitly.

### Concepts related to the facade basics

- For module root rules see @#module-roots (Module roots).
- For page purpose and dependency non-activation see @#entry-runtime-and-fragments (Active-root runtime and fragments).
